<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>RQ2: Signal Validity of Attention from Symptom Steps</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the identification of symptoms via NLL, RQ2 evaluates whether attention from symptom steps provides useful guidance for prioritizing upstream failure-relevant steps. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.3. RQ2: Signal Validity of Attention from Symptom Steps ‣ 4. Evaluation ‣ MASPrism: Lightweight Failure Attribution for Multi-Agent Systems Using Prefill-Stage Signals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(a) and Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3. RQ2: Signal Validity of Attention from Symptom Steps ‣ 4. Evaluation ‣ MASPrism: Lightweight Failure Attribution for Multi-Agent Systems Using Prefill-Stage Signals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, annotated source steps receive higher attention mass from symptom steps than both neighboring source steps and randomly sampled earlier steps across both Who&amp;When splits. In the Hand-Crafted (HC) split, which contains longer traces with 51.60 steps on average, the difference is statistically significant against both GT-neighbor (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1.90\times 10^{-9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1.90</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1.90\times 10^{-9}</annotation></semantics></math>) and random earlier-step (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=9.82\times 10^{-9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>9.82</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=9.82\times 10^{-9}</annotation></semantics></math>) baselines, with a mean difference of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.24\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.24</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.24\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>. These results indicate that attention from symptom steps provides an empirical routing signal for upstream candidate prioritization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The same trend appears in the Algorithm-Generated (AG) split. The comparison against GT-neighbor steps remains significant (<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=3.31\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3.31</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=3.31\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>), while the separation from random earlier steps is weaker but still positive (<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=4.98\times 10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4.98</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=4.98\times 10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>). This weaker separation is expected because AG traces are shorter on average, which reduces the number of earlier alternatives and makes random earlier steps more likely to be near the annotated source. Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.3. RQ2: Signal Validity of Attention from Symptom Steps ‣ 4. Evaluation ‣ MASPrism: Lightweight Failure Attribution for Multi-Agent Systems Using Prefill-Stage Signals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(b) further shows that annotated source steps are often ranked near the top among earlier steps. Together, these results support the use of attention from symptom steps as a practical signal for selecting and ranking upstream candidates in the diagnosis pipeline.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07509v1/rq2_attention_main_hc_ag.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/179;" width="467" height="179"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5. </span>Attention from symptom steps on Who&amp;When.
Left: attention mass received by GT source steps, GT-source neighbors, and random earlier steps.
Right: ECDF of the normalized rank of the GT source step among earlier candidates, where lower is better.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2. </span>Paired one-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank tests for attention mass. <math id="S4.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is the per-chain difference between the GT source step and the comparison step.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T2.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Split</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Comparison</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T2.5.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.5.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Mean <math id="S4.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
